[x] Evaluate existing agent runners before extracting our own =evaluate-agent-runners $Before extracting the sandbox into its own tool, check whether an existing runner already does this better. Requirements: rootless podman on our own hardware, no proprietary cloud; headless unattended runs; several harnesses, at least Claude Code and pi; output only as git commits, no credentials in the container; git submodules; OpenTelemetry; spend caps. Candidates include Dagger's container-use. What is ours regardless: bottom-up submodule landing, the stopping-is-good outcome semantics, standard telemetry attributes. Outcome: adopt, build on top of, or extract ours, recorded here with the reasons.  FINDINGS 2026-09-25, recommendation pending the human's call: extract ours, borrowing ideas. Dagger container-use, Apache 2.0, marked experimental: the agent runs on the host and calls the container through MCP tools, so the harness keeps its own host tools and credentials; isolation is weaker than ours, where the whole harness is inside. obra/packnplay, MIT: closest in shape, the agent runs inside, podman works via DOCKER_CMD=podman, and it reads devcontainer config; but it mounts the main repo .git read-write, which is the hook and config escape path we rejected for worktrees, mounts git credentials and ssh keys by default, is interactive-first, and says nothing of submodules. Built-in harness sandboxes, Claude Code bubblewrap and Codex Landlock, are per-harness process confinement, not a shared runner. Surveyed via the 2026-05 coding-agent-sandboxes list by wincent: nothing found combines headless queue runs, stopping-is-good outcomes, bottom-up submodule landing and standard telemetry. Worth borrowing: podman's libkrun runtime, podman run --runtime krun, gives microVM isolation with the same interface, an upgrade path without leaving podman.  DECIDED 2026-09-25 by the human: extract ours, after sandbox-driver-split. It is good enough to use now, and nothing surveyed fits the requirements.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T16:08 %2026-09-25T16:13 #01a0dad3-fe6c-7f11-b7a1-aad22dff5e11
[x] Summarize running and finished runs on demand =sandbox-status $scripts/agent-status, for all runs or one id: state, elapsed, idle seconds since the last transcript event, event count, and the last few tool calls. Stuck means the container is running and idle for more than N minutes; the transcript's modification time is the heartbeat, so nothing hooks into the harness. Each harness adapter owns summarizing its own event stream, since claude stream-json and pi json mode differ; the status command stays generic. This is the on-demand bridge: the lasting answer is telemetry, sandbox-telemetry, which serves the same questions from the collector across sandbox and interactive sessions and every exporting harness. Found 2026-09-25: the only live signal was podman ps, while the transcript already held the whole story.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T16:26 %2026-09-25T16:30 #01a0dae3-f79f-7601-8d06-00c5b01ae657
[x] Move the sandbox image to a root Containerfile =sandbox-root-containerfile $podman build finds a root Containerfile with no flags, so the convention is simply the repo's default Containerfile; any repo that has one works with the sandbox. Move agents/Containerfile to the platform root and add a root .containerignore holding a single asterisk: code arrives through the mount, and without it every build ships clearhead-core/target and node_modules as context. One environment for the superproject; per-submodule images plus compose were rejected as ceremony, since scoping a run to one submodule needs only the prompt.  ADDED 2026-09-25: pin the base image by digest, FROM docker.io/library/archlinux@sha256:..., not latest. Changing one package name rebuilt every layer from that day's Arch and broke on unrelated upstream changes: a LuaRocks 3.13.0 crash and npm blocking Claude Code's install script by default. Keep additions in their own layers so they do not invalidate the pacman layer. Also fix the Claude Code install so it works under npm's new default: allow its install script explicitly.  IMPLEMENTED 2026-09-25 by Codex, gpt-5.6-sol, in run 20260925-170315; reviewed by Claude, verdict land; the image build was verified on the host. Its commits were lost to an agent-land bug before harvest and reapplied from the reviewed diff in 2834309.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 %2026-09-25T17:43 #01a0dab3-2d6b-7c92-9bef-3457a5bf4c4e
[x] Snapshot the harness into each run =sandbox-harness-snapshot $agents/ is mounted live into the running container, so editing agents/loop mid-run can corrupt a shell that reads it incrementally. agent-run copies the harness into the run directory and mounts that copy read-only: each run keeps exactly the harness it started with, which also makes runs reproducible.  IMPLEMENTED 2026-09-25 by Codex, gpt-5.6-sol, in run 20260925-170315; reviewed by Claude, verdict land; the image build was verified on the host. Its commits were lost to an agent-land bug before harvest and reapplied from the reviewed diff in 2834309.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 %2026-09-25T17:43 #01a0dab3-2db5-76e3-b433-81d7e9963547
[x] Pin the sandbox image's packages to a dated Arch snapshot =sandbox-dated-snapshot $DECIDED 2026-09-25 with the human: stay on Arch and pin packages to a dated Arch Linux Archive snapshot; revisit only if dated snapshots prove not good enough. Nix, not Debian, is the base to consider then: Debian's Rust, Node, Neovim and tree-sitter are too old here, so half the toolchain would come from upstream installers with a pin each. Why: pinning the base image by digest does not pin packages, because the first layer runs pacman -Syu, which installs whatever Arch ships that day; a cross-vendor review of the root Containerfile found this, and the action that asked only for a digest was the wrong fix. Do: in the root Containerfile, before pacman -Syu, write /etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist as Server=https://archive.archlinux.org/repos/2026/09/25/\$repo/os/\$arch, matching the base digest's era; 2026-09-25 is the date whose packages were built and verified on the host. Updating becomes a deliberate change of date. Keep Arch parity with the host: same Rust, Node and tree-sitter. Verify by building on the host, since podman is not available inside the sandbox. Depends on the root Containerfile run landing first. Implemented in fa949a5: the root Containerfile now replaces /etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist with the 2026/09/25 Arch Linux Archive URL before pacman -Syu, so package resolution is tied to the host-verified snapshot while retaining Arch toolchain parity. Verified with the full clearhead-core gate and clearhead doctor. The image build remains unverified in this sandbox because podman is unavailable, as expected by the action.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T17:39 %2026-09-26T01:33 #01a0db26-bb79-7a01-bef8-220622a4819e
[x] Land run 20260925-171333 after its Codex review =land-run-171333 $Claude, claude-sonnet-5, implemented charters-default-new in clearhead-core as 2d5efcc: loading, init, jot's path-derived id, the add action hint, the doctor warning and the CLI README. It hit its 6 dollar cap before committing the completion; the orchestrator committed that as 8cf39c8 in the run's clone. Already harvested into platform and clearhead-core as branch agent/20260925-171333. A read-only Codex review was running at handoff; its findings are in ~/agent-runs/20260925-171333/reviews/pi-codex.jsonl, final assistant message_end. Next: read the verdict; if land, scripts/agent-land 20260925-171333 with ~/.local/bin on PATH for check-jsonschema, then push; if findings block, a follow-up work run. Note that sandbox Claude runs use the subscription default model, Sonnet, since no model is set; decide that explicitly.  REVIEW VERDICT, Codex: ### Findings  - **Should-fix** — `crates/clearhead-cli/src/cli/action.rs:100,1337`: after adding the action, `warn_if_charter_is_new` reloads the entire workspace and silently suppresses loading errors. `resolve_acts_file` already loads and then discards the target charter in normal resolution paths. Return the resolved charter metadata alongside the path and emit the reminder from that, avoiding duplicate I/O and ensuring the required notice is not silently lost.  - **Should-fix** — `crates/clearhead-cli/src/cli/action.rs:1355`, `crates/clearhead-core/src/workspace/store/doctor.rs:248`, `crates/clearhead-cli/src/cli/init.rs:130`: generated activation commands interpolate aliases or titles without shell quoting. A charter such as `Someday Work` produces `clearhead update charter Someday Work --state active`, which is not executable as shown. Use a safely quoted selector or stable identifier, and add coverage for names containing spaces or shell metacharacters.  Validation: the full gate passes from a writable archive with `CLEARHEAD_SPEC_DIR=/job/work/specifications`. The read-only review checkout itself cannot run tests that write into their current directory.  **Verdict: land after fixes.** LANDED 2026-09-25: Follow-up in clearhead-core 57e403b and 4f52750 reuses the resolved Charter metadata (including document-only --file targets), prints stable UUID activation selectors, and tests shell-unsafe full titles. Gemini independently reviewed the full branch and final delta, verdict land. The repository-wide landing gate exposed a conformance fixture with an implicit New root; aebc88e explicitly activates that fixture, separately reviewed by Gemini. scripts/agent-land 20260925-171333 then passed scripts/validate-pinned and landed the branch in platform as 9103e2d. No further fixes required.$ !1 ^2026-09-25T17:48 %2026-09-25T18:55 #01a0db2f-1225-7c73-8f43-a8684527d40e
